---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { ArrowUpRight, Thermometer, CloudRain, Snowflake, Info } from '@lucide/astro';
const maps = [
  { name: 'Temperature', file: 'djf-temperature.gif', alt: 'CPC temperature outlook issued November 19, 2026 for December 2026 through February 2027: warmth favored across the northern two-thirds of the contiguous U.S.; near-normal favored in parts of the Southeast; equal chances in parts of the Southwest.', note: 'Above-normal temperatures are favored across the northern two-thirds of the contiguous U.S. Near-normal is favored in parts of the Southeast; parts of the Southwest have equal chances.' },
  { name: 'Precipitation', file: 'djf-precipitation.gif', alt: 'CPC precipitation outlook issued November 19, 2026 for December 2026 through February 2027: wetter favored in southwestern Oregon, California, the Southwest and much of the southern and eastern U.S.; drier favored in the northern Rockies and parts of the Great Lakes.', note: 'Wetter conditions are favored in southwestern Oregon, California, the Southwest and much of the southern and eastern U.S. Drier conditions are favored in the northern Rockies and parts of the Great Lakes.' },
];
---
<Layout title="U.S. Winter Outlook 2026–27" active="winter" description="NOAA CPC's November 19 outlook for December 2026–February 2027: dated temperature and precipitation maps, regional interpretation, equal chances and snowfall limits.">
  <div class="page-shell subpage lifecycle-page">
    <div class="page-intro"><div><span class="eyebrow">CPC ISSUE NOVEMBER 19, 2026 · REVIEWED NOVEMBER 21</span><h1>U.S. winter<br /><span>2026–27.</span></h1><p>December 2026–February 2027: the temperature and precipitation outlook, region by region.</p></div></div>
    <div class="content-notice"><Info size={18} /><div><strong>A dated outlook, not a storm forecast.</strong><p>The maps and interpretation below preserve the November 19, 2026 issue for DJF 2026–27. They are reviewed manually and do not update with the dashboard's ENSO feed. The next scheduled seasonal outlook is December 17, 2026; check the <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/predictions/long_range/">current CPC outlook</a> after that release for subsequent revisions.</p></div></div>
    <nav class="winter-contents" aria-label="Winter outlook topics"><a href="#winter-maps-heading">Saved maps</a> · <a href="#november-changes-heading">What changed in November?</a> · <a href="#regional-heading">Regional reading</a> · <a href="#probability-examples-heading">Probability examples</a> · <a href="#forecast-horizon-heading">Choose a forecast horizon</a> · <a href="#mjo-enso-heading">MJO and ENSO</a></nav>
    <section class="lifecycle-section" aria-labelledby="winter-maps-heading">
      <h2 id="winter-maps-heading">Two maps. Different questions.</h2>
      <p class="body-copy">Temperature refers to the three-month average; precipitation refers to the three-month total. Both compare with the 1991–2020 climate reference period. Neither map predicts the timing of individual storms or cold spells.</p>
      <div class="outlook-maps">
        {maps.map(map => <figure>
          <h3>{map.name}</h3>
          <a href={'/images/outlooks/2026-11/' + map.file} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label={'Open full-size ' + map.name.toLowerCase() + ' outlook map'}>
            <img src={'/images/outlooks/2026-11/' + map.file} alt={map.alt} width="3300" height="2550" />
          </a>
          <figcaption>{map.note} <a href={'/images/outlooks/2026-11/' + map.file} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Full-size map <ArrowUpRight size={13} /></a></figcaption>
        </figure>)}
      </div>
      <p class="figure-note">Original maps: NOAA / CPC, issued November 19, 2026; valid December 2026–February 2027. Copies saved November 21, 2026 with the original dates and legends intact.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="lifecycle-section" aria-labelledby="november-changes-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow">SAME TARGET: DECEMBER 2026–FEBRUARY 2027</span>
      <h2 id="november-changes-heading">What changed in the November outlook?</h2>
      <p class="body-copy">CPC's November 19, 2026 discussion explicitly increases the odds of above-normal precipitation for California, the Desert Southwest and the East Coast compared with its October 15 issue. This compares two forecasts for the same DJF winter, not November weather with December weather. It is a forecast revision, not evidence that the extra rain has already fallen.</p>
      <div class="regional-grid"><article><h3>California and the Southwest</h3><p>The wet tilt strengthened. This remains a probability for the three-month precipitation total, not a Sierra snowfall forecast or a prediction of individual atmospheric rivers.</p></article><article><h3>East Coast and Southeast</h3><p>Wetter odds increased along the East Coast. Drought relief is possible, but rainfall must reach depleted soil and water stores; a wet category is not a flood probability.</p></article><article><h3>Read the Northwest locally</h3><p>Southwestern Oregon favors wetter conditions while Washington and northern Oregon show equal chances for precipitation. The northern Rockies favor drier conditions. A single “dry Northwest” label loses this distinction.</p></article></div>
      <p class="body-copy">The temperature map favors warmth across the northern two-thirds of the contiguous U.S.; parts of the Southeast favor near-normal temperatures and parts of the Southwest have equal chances. A warm seasonal average still allows cold spells. <a href="/references/winter-update-2026-11-21.txt">Dated source record and saved-map checksums</a>.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="regional-section" aria-labelledby="regional-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow">DJF 2026–27 · NOVEMBER ISSUE</span><h2 id="regional-heading">A regional lens</h2>
      <div class="regional-grid">
        <article><CloudRain size={24} /><h3>California & Southwest</h3><p>The outlook favors above-normal precipitation across much of the region. Temperature odds vary: parts of the Southwest have equal chances. Wetter does not automatically mean more Sierra snow.</p><a href="/impacts/california-snow-vs-rain/">California snow and rain</a></article>
        <article><Thermometer size={24} /><h3>Northern United States</h3><p>Above-normal seasonal temperatures are favored across much of the northern tier. Below-normal precipitation is favored in the northern Rockies and parts of the Great Lakes. A warmer average still allows cold outbreaks.</p></article>
        <article><CloudRain size={24} /><h3>Southeast & Gulf Coast</h3><p>The outlook favors above-normal precipitation across much of the Southeast. Parts of the region favor near-normal temperatures, rather than a blanket forecast of a colder winter. For water supply, separate the wet outlook from <a href="/learn/el-nino-and-drought-recovery/">observed drought recovery</a>.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="explanation-grid">
      <div><h2>Probability is not magnitude.</h2><p>A 50% chance of above-normal precipitation means a 50% chance of landing in the wettest of three climatological categories. It does not mean 50% more rain, or rain on half the days. Shading describes category probability; local totals remain uncertain.</p></div>
      <div><h2>Equal chances is not near-normal.</h2><p>EC means no favored category: above-, near- and below-normal each have about a one-third chance. A near-normal forecast instead favors the middle category. White EC areas do not promise ordinary weather or an absence of extremes.</p></div>
    </section>
    <section class="lifecycle-section" aria-labelledby="probability-examples-heading">
      <h2 id="probability-examples-heading">How can 40% be the favored outcome?</h2>
      <p class="body-copy">CPC’s seasonal map legend gives a useful reading exercise: at the 40% above-normal boundary, the three probabilities are approximately 40% above, 33.3% near and 26.7% below normal. Above normal is the largest individual category even though the other two together have a 60% chance. “Favored” does not require a majority.</p>
      <div class="regional-grid"><article><h3>40% above normal</h3><p>The wettest or warmest category is favored. This is a category probability, not a 40% increase in rain or temperature.</p></article><article><h3>60% outside that category</h3><p>The remainder includes both near and below normal. It is not a 60% chance of the opposite extreme.</p></article></div>
      <p class="body-copy">Source: <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/predictions/multi_season/13_seasonal_outlooks/color/shortlegend.gif">CPC official seasonal forecast legend</a>, undated, checked November 23, 2026. This is a legend-based exercise, not a forecast for a named region. The near-normal probability is not fixed at one-third for every probability range, and this regional legend does not define ENSO phase odds.</p>
      <div class="lifecycle-answer"><h3>Can I combine the warm and wet maps?</h3><p class="body-copy">Two separate maps do not supply the probability of both outcomes together. In a hypothetical case with a 50% chance of above-normal temperature and a 50% chance of above-normal precipitation for the same location and season, their overlap could range from 0% to 50%. Multiplying to obtain 25% would require an independence assumption those two maps do not establish. These are invented probabilities for a mathematical exercise, not current CPC odds.</p></div>
      <p class="body-copy">For a combined warm-and-wet question, seek a product or analysis that explicitly estimates the joint outcome. Preserve the separate maps’ valid dates and variables rather than turning them into an unsupported combined forecast.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="winter-layout"><div><span class="eyebrow">FROM SEASON TO STORM</span><h2>Rain is not snow.</h2><p class="body-copy">Seasonal precipitation includes rain and the water equivalent of snow. Elevation, storm snow levels and later melt determine the stored snowpack. For a resort visit, use local forecasts at the relevant elevation. ENSO is one input to the seasonal assessment alongside other climate patterns, forecast tools and long-term trends.</p><a class="primary-button" href="https://www.weather.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Local forecasts & warnings <ArrowUpRight size={17} /></a></div><div class="winter-side"><Snowflake size={36} stroke-width={1} /><h3>Read the right season.</h3><p>DJF means December–February. NDJ means November–January. Adjacent outlooks overlap but cover different periods. Use the dates printed on each map.</p><a href="/forecast/#strength-heading">ENSO phase and strength</a></div></section>
    <section class="lifecycle-section" aria-labelledby="forecast-horizon-heading">
      <h2 id="forecast-horizon-heading">Which forecast should I use?</h2>
      <p class="body-copy">Match the product's valid period to your question. A three-month outlook and a shorter outlook can favor different categories without contradicting each other: a wet fortnight can occur within a dry season. Read the issue date, target dates and legend each time.</p>
      <div class="lifecycle-answer"><h3>A whole winter or a calendar month</h3><p class="body-copy">Use <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/predictions/long_range/">CPC seasonal outlooks</a> for three-month temperature and precipitation categories, or the <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/predictions/30day/">monthly outlook</a> for the named month. Neither gives a daily sequence, storm count or resort snowfall total.</p></div>
      <div class="lifecycle-answer" id="seasonal-lead-example"><h3>“0.5 month” is a lead label, not a half-month forecast</h3><p class="body-copy">In the CPC seasonal index checked November 23, 2026, “0.5 Month Outlook” labels December–January–February 2026–27; “1.5 Month” labels January–February–March 2027; “2.5 Month” labels February–March–April 2027. All three cover three-month seasons. The increasing number describes lead time, while the printed season gives the valid period. This preserves the November issue’s labels as a teaching example; later issues will have different targets.</p></div>
      <div class="lifecycle-answer"><h3>Weeks 3–4</h3><p class="body-copy">The <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/predictions/WK34/">CPC Week 3–4 outlook</a> covers the two-week average temperature and accumulated precipitation for its printed target dates. It is not a separate forecast for every day. CPC labels the precipitation product experimental; check the current product notes.</p></div>
      <div class="lifecycle-answer"><h3>About one to two weeks ahead</h3><p class="body-copy">Consult the <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/predictions/610day/">6–10-day</a> and <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/predictions/814day/">8–14-day outlooks</a> for temperature and precipitation category probabilities over those windows. Their periods overlap. A wet-category probability is not the chance of rain on a particular day.</p></div>
      <div class="lifecycle-answer"><h3>A trip, a storm or a safety decision</h3><p class="body-copy">Use the <a href="https://www.weather.gov/">local National Weather Service forecast and warnings</a> for your location and elevation. Check snow levels, wind, timing and road or resort reports as the event approaches. A seasonal ENSO outlook cannot provide those details.</p></div>
    </section>
    <section class="lifecycle-section" aria-labelledby="winter-variability-heading"><h2 id="winter-variability-heading">Why can similar El Niño winters turn out differently?</h2><p class="body-copy">ENSO changes the background odds, while internal atmospheric variability still changes storm tracks and precipitation. In model experiments, different ensemble members can produce different winter precipitation patterns despite similar ENSO conditions. Averaging them helps reveal a predictable signal; that average is not a guaranteed map of the winter that will occur.</p><p class="body-copy">See NOAA's <a href="https://www.climate.gov/news-features/blogs/enso/how-does-el-nino-influence-winter-precipitation-over-united-states">November 30, 2023 explanation of winter precipitation variability</a> (archived). Its historical experiments explain uncertainty; its old outlook is not a forecast for 2026–27. This forecast-selection guidance was added November 12, 2026, separately from the dated maps above.</p></section>
    <section class="lifecycle-section" aria-labelledby="mjo-enso-heading">
      <h2 id="mjo-enso-heading">MJO versus ENSO: why can winter weather change within a season?</h2>
      <p class="body-copy">The Madden–Julian Oscillation (MJO) is an eastward-moving disturbance of tropical clouds, rainfall, winds and pressure. MJO events typically recur on a roughly 30–60-day timescale, with multiple events possible in a season. That average is not a countdown to the next storm or a guarantee of useful forecast lead time.</p>
      <p class="body-copy">ENSO provides a more persistent Pacific ocean–atmosphere background that can last several seasons. The MJO operates within a season: its areas of enhanced and suppressed tropical rainfall change atmospheric heating and can influence the jet stream and North American weather. A seasonal temperature tendency can therefore coexist with shorter cold or warm spells.</p>
      <div class="lifecycle-answer"><h3>A possible influence, not a local forecast</h3><p class="body-copy">The MJO is one contributor among several. An MJO phase alone does not specify a city's temperature, a storm's track or a cold outbreak's cause. Its strength, evolution and the wider atmospheric state matter. Use the <a href="#forecast-horizon-heading">forecast guide above</a> to select the appropriate outlook, then consult <a href="https://www.weather.gov/">local forecasts and warnings</a> for a particular event.</p></div>
      <p class="body-copy">Background: NOAA's <a href="https://www.climate.gov/news-features/blogs/enso/what-mjo-and-why-do-we-care">December 31, 2014 MJO explainer</a> (archived). For monitoring, use <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/precip/CWlink/MJO/mjo.shtml">CPC's MJO indices, forecasts and weekly discussion</a> and check each product's valid date. This explanation was reviewed November 12, 2026; it does not report a current MJO phase or revise the dated winter maps.</p>
    </section>
    <div class="source-callout"><Info size={20} /><div><strong>Source and review dates</strong><p>NOAA / CPC seasonal discussion and maps issued November 19, 2026; checked and saved November 21, 2026. Target: DJF 2026–27; reference: 1991–2020. Next scheduled seasonal issue: December 17, 2026. The <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/predictions/long_range/seasonal.php?lead=1" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CPC map selector</a> rolls forward; the copies above preserve this issue.</p></div><a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/predictions/long_range/fxus05.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CPC discussion <ArrowUpRight size={15} /></a></div>
    <p class="body-copy">More regional context: <a href="/impacts/">ENSO impact guides</a> · <a href="/impacts/pacific-northwest-winter/">Pacific Northwest snowpack</a> · <a href="/forecast/enso-neutral/">Neutral versus equal chances</a>.</p>
  </div>
</Layout>
<style>
  .winter-contents { line-height:2; margin:24px 0; }
  .winter-contents a { color:var(--green); }
  #november-changes-heading,#winter-maps-heading,#regional-heading { scroll-margin-top:110px; }
  #probability-examples-heading, #seasonal-lead-example, #forecast-horizon-heading, #winter-variability-heading, #mjo-enso-heading { scroll-margin-top: 100px; }
  .outlook-maps { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 28px; margin-top: 24px; }
  figure { margin: 0; min-width: 0; }
  figure h3 { font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 12px; }
  figure img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; aspect-ratio: 22 / 17; }
  figcaption { margin-top: 12px; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--muted); }
  figcaption a { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 4px; color: var(--green); }
  .regional-grid a, .winter-side a { display: inline-block; color: var(--green); margin-top: 14px; font-size: 13px; }
  .winter-side { border-radius: 8px; }
  @media (max-width: 760px) { .outlook-maps { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
</style>
